# Weather theory and services: Aviation Weather Handbook FAA-H-8083-28A; AIM chapter 7; PHAK chapter 12.
# ACS I.C. METAR, TAF and winds-aloft strings are written out in the stem.
ITEMS = [
    # ---------- Practice Test 1 ----------
    S(1, 2, "Virga", "You see virga, streaks of rain evaporating below a cloud base, near the airport on a hot day. What hazard can it signal?",
      ["Strong downdrafts, including dry microbursts", "Freezing rain at the surface", "Advection fog forming within the hour"],
      "Rain evaporating below a high cloud base cools the air, which can accelerate downward as a dry microburst (AIM 7-1-24)."),
    S(1, 2, "Wind shear alerts", "What does a low-level wind shear alert system (LLWAS) at an airport do?",
      ["Compares winds from sensors around the airport to detect shear near the runways", "Measures turbulence in the clouds above the airport with radar", "Forecasts wind shear and microbursts for the airport over the next 24 hours"],
      "LLWAS uses anemometers around the airport to spot wind differences that signal wind shear or microbursts, and controllers pass the alerts to pilots (AIM 4-3-7, 7-1-24)."),
    S(1, 2, "METAR", "METAR KABC 121853Z 28015G28KT 1 1/2SM -SHRA BR BKN007 OVC015 12/11 A2971. What is the ceiling?",
      ["700 feet AGL", "1,500 feet AGL", "15,000 feet AGL"],
      "The ceiling is the lowest broken or overcast layer. BKN007 is a broken layer at 700 feet above ground level."),
    S(1, 2, "TAF", "In a terminal aerodrome forecast, what does TEMPO mean?",
      ["Temporary changes, each lasting less than an hour and in total less than half the period", "A permanent change that occurs gradually over the period", "A change that begins at the stated time and lasts the rest of the forecast period"],
      "TEMPO forecasts temporary fluctuations expected to last less than an hour each and less than half of the period in total (FAA-H-8083-28A). PROB30 gives a 30 percent probability."),
    S(1, 2, "SIGMET", "Which condition would be covered by a non-convective SIGMET?",
      ["Severe turbulence not caused by thunderstorms", "Moderate icing over a wide area", "Sustained surface winds of 30 knots"],
      "SIGMETs cover severe icing and severe or extreme turbulence not caused by thunderstorms, dust or sandstorms lowering visibility below 3 miles, and volcanic ash. Moderate conditions are G-AIRMET items (FAA-H-8083-28A)."),
    S(1, 3, "Pressure and wind", "In the Northern Hemisphere you fly with a steady wind from your left. What does that tell you about pressure ahead?",
      ["You are flying toward lower pressure, so the altimeter may read too high", "You are flying toward higher pressure, so the altimeter may read too low", "Nothing, since wind direction is not related to pressure"],
      "With your back to the wind in the Northern Hemisphere, low pressure is on your left. A wind from the left therefore puts the low ahead. Flying from high to low pressure without resetting the altimeter, you are lower than indicated."),
    # ---------- Practice Test 2 ----------
    S(2, 2, "Microbursts", "You fly into a microburst on final. What is the usual order of effects?",
      ["A headwind with a climb, then a downdraft, then a strong tailwind", "A tailwind with a sink, then a headwind with a climb", "A steady downdraft only, with no change in wind"],
      "Entering a microburst gives a performance-increasing headwind, then the core downdraft, then a tailwind that cuts airspeed. Downdrafts can reach about 6,000 feet per minute (AIM 7-1-24)."),
    S(2, 2, "Mountain flying", "In mountain winds, how should you approach a ridge?",
      ["At about a 45 degree angle, so you can turn away easily", "Straight at it, so you cross in the shortest time", "Parallel to it on the downwind side"],
      "Crossing a ridge at an angle of about 45 degrees lets you turn away quickly toward lower terrain if downdrafts are stronger than expected (FAA Tips on Mountain Flying, FAA-P-8740-60)."),
    S(2, 2, "Frost", "Why must frost be removed from the wings before takeoff?",
      ["It disrupts airflow and can raise the stall speed and cut lift", "It adds so much weight the airplane exceeds maximum takeoff weight", "It blocks the static ports and stops the airspeed indicator"],
      "Even a thin layer of frost roughens the wing surface, spoiling the airflow. That reduces lift and increases drag and stall speed. 14 CFR 91.527 and AC 91-74 say to remove it before flight."),
    S(2, 3, "Winds aloft", "A winds and temperatures aloft forecast for FL340 reads 7525-45. What wind is forecast?",
      ["250 degrees true at 125 knots, minus 45 C", "750 degrees true at 25 knots, minus 45 C", "250 degrees true at 25 knots, minus 45 C"],
      "When speed is 100 to 199 knots, 50 is added to the direction code. Subtract 50 from 75 to get 25 (250 degrees) and add 100 to the speed: 125 knots. Temperatures above 24,000 feet are all negative (FAA-H-8083-28A)."),
    S(2, 2, "Cloud types", "In the morning you see altocumulus castellanus, turret-shaped middle clouds. What does that suggest?",
      ["Instability aloft and possible thunderstorms later in the day", "A stable atmosphere with smooth air all day", "Fog and low stratus forming at the surface by the evening"],
      "Castellanus clouds show the air at middle levels is unstable. They often come before afternoon thunderstorms (FAA-H-8083-28A)."),
    S(2, 3, "Automated stations", "A METAR from an automated station includes the remark AO2. What does it mean?",
      ["The station has a precipitation discriminator", "The station has no precipitation sensor", "The report was edited by a weather observer"],
      "AO1 marks an automated station without a precipitation discriminator; AO2 means it can tell rain from snow (FAA-H-8083-28A)."),
    # ---------- Drill only ----------
    S(0, 2, "METAR", "A METAR shows SQ. What weather is that?",
      ["A squall: a sudden wind increase of at least 16 knots, to 22 knots or more, lasting a minute or longer", "A steady wind of 16 knots or more from one direction, with gusts to at least 22 knots", "A line of thunderstorms within 10 miles of the station, moving toward it"],
      "SQ reports a squall, a sudden onset of strong wind rising by at least 16 knots and sustained at 22 knots or more for at least one minute (FAA-H-8083-28A)."),
    S(0, 3, "Ice and stall warning", "Why can an iced airplane stall without its stall warning sounding first?",
      ["Ice raises the stall speed and lowers the critical angle of attack below the warning setting", "Ice blocks the static ports, so the airspeed indicator reads too high near the stall", "Ice on the stall warning vane makes the horn sound only at a lower angle of attack than normal"],
      "Ice roughens the wing so it stalls at a lower angle of attack and higher speed than clean. The stall warning is set for a clean wing and may not sound in time (AC 91-74B)."),
    S(0, 2, "Thunderstorms", "According to the AIM, which thunderstorms should be regarded as extremely hazardous?",
      ["Any with tops at 35,000 feet or higher, seen visually or on radar", "Any with tops below 20,000 feet", "Only those with lightning that can be seen from the airplane at night"],
      "The AIM (7-1-29) says to regard as extremely hazardous any thunderstorm with tops of 35,000 feet or higher, whether you see it or it shows on radar."),
    S(0, 2, "Wind shear", "Where is wind shear likely as a warm front approaches?",
      ["Below the frontal surface, sometimes before the front reaches the airport", "Only after the front has passed, behind the surface wind shift", "Only in the jet stream above about 18,000 feet MSL"],
      "With a warm front, the shear zone is where the warm air rides over the cold air near the ground. It can exist ahead of the surface front for several hours."),
    S(0, 2, "Turbulence", "Where is clear air turbulence most likely near a jet stream?",
      ["On the polar side, near a sharp curve or trough", "On the equatorial side, far from any trough", "Directly in the jet core at its slowest point"],
      "Clear air turbulence with a jet stream is most common on its polar (low-pressure) side and where the jet curves sharply around a deep trough (FAA-H-8083-28A)."),
    S(0, 2, "Icing", "Under which conditions does clear ice most often form?",
      ["Large supercooled drops at temperatures from 0 to about -10 C", "Small supercooled drops at temperatures colder than -20 C", "Dry snow at any temperature below freezing"],
      "Small drops freeze on impact as rime. Large supercooled drops, typically from 0 to about -10 C, freeze slowly and flow back before freezing into clear, hard ice."),
    S(0, 2, "Haze", "Flying toward the sun in haze, how does your slant visibility usually compare with the reported surface visibility?",
      ["It is often worse", "It is always better", "It is always the same"],
      "Sunlight scattering in haze can cut slant visibility, especially looking toward the sun, below what a ground observer reports (FAA-H-8083-28A)."),
    S(0, 2, "METAR remarks", "A METAR remark reads PK WND 28045/15. What does it mean?",
      ["A peak wind from 280 degrees at 45 knots at 15 minutes past the hour", "Wind from 280 degrees at 15 knots, gusting to 45", "Wind from 280 degrees at a steady 45 knots for the last 15 minutes"],
      "PK WND gives the peak wind since the last report: direction, speed and the time it occurred, here minute 15 of the hour (FAA-H-8083-28A)."),
    S(0, 2, "Stability", "A cold air mass moves over a much warmer surface. What weather do you expect?",
      ["Cumuliform clouds, showers and good visibility outside them", "Stratus, steady drizzle and poor visibility", "Smooth air with widespread fog and low stratus"],
      "Heating from below makes the cold air unstable. That brings cumulus clouds, turbulence and showers, with good visibility between them."),
    S(0, 2, "Surface winds", "Why do surface winds cross isobars toward low pressure while winds aloft blow along them?",
      ["Surface friction slows the wind and weakens the Coriolis force", "The Coriolis force is stronger at the surface", "Warm air near the surface always flows toward the low"],
      "Aloft the pressure gradient and Coriolis force balance and the wind follows the isobars. Near the ground, friction slows the wind and reduces the Coriolis force, so the pressure gradient turns it toward the low."),
    S(0, 2, "Cloud types", "What do mammatus clouds, pouches hanging under a thunderstorm anvil, indicate?",
      ["Severe turbulence in and near the storm", "A storm that is dissipating and safe to pass under", "Light rain with no turbulence"],
      "Mammatus pouches form in strong sinking air under the anvil and mark a vigorous storm with severe turbulence (FAA-H-8083-28A)."),
    S(0, 3, "Freezing level", "The surface temperature is 10 degrees C. Using the standard lapse rate of 2 degrees C per 1,000 feet, about where is the freezing level?",
      ["About 5,000 feet above the surface", "About 2,500 feet above the surface", "About 10,000 feet above the surface"],
      "Temperature falls about 2 degrees C per 1,000 feet in the standard atmosphere. Losing 10 degrees takes about 10 / 2 x 1,000 = 5,000 feet."),
    S(0, 2, "Mountain winds", "Late at night in a mountain valley with calm weather overhead, which way does the local wind usually flow?",
      ["Down the slopes into the valley", "Up the slopes out of the valley", "Across the valley from one ridge to the other"],
      "At night the slopes cool and the dense cold air drains downhill as a mountain breeze. In the daytime heated slopes produce an upslope valley breeze."),
]
